.TH OVCP 8 "2026-07-25" "ovcp" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ovcp \- OpenVPN Control Plane: web UI, native PKI, and lifecycle management for an OpenVPN server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ovcp
[\fB\-data\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-no\-color\fR]
[\fB\-json\fR]
[\fB\-debug\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ovcp
is a single static binary that manages one OpenVPN server: a built\-in
certificate authority (no easy\-rsa), client profile export, server
configuration, and a web admin UI. It talks to the running
.BR openvpn (8)
process over its management \fIunix socket\fR (disconnects only \(en
connected\-client status is read from its periodic status file instead, see
\fBFILES\fR) and owns everything else through files in its data directory.
.B ovcp
runs as root, is the sole owner of the PKI and configuration, and starts
openvpn itself as a reaped foreground child \(en the same on the host, under
systemd, and inside the container.
.PP
The CA private key is encrypted at rest; a passphrase held only in memory
gates every signing operation (see \fBSECURITY\fR).
.PP
This page is also rendered into the web UI's \fBDocs\fR tab at build time
\(en one source, readable with or without a terminal.
.PP
This page is the complete runtime reference. Building and packaging are
documented in \fBBUILD.md\fR; internal design in \fBARCHITECTURE.md\fR.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
.BR openvpn (8)
on \fBPATH\fR \(en ovcp starts, stops, and controls it but never bundles
it. Linux with \fI/dev/net/tun\fR; root or \fBCAP_NET_ADMIN\fR for openvpn
to bring the tunnel up (see \fBPRIVILEGES\fR).
.PP
Building ovcp itself \(en compiler and toolchain requirements,
cross\-compiling, packaging, and the container image \(en is documented in
\fBBUILD.md\fR, not here.
.SH SECURITY
Tier\-2 CA model: the CA private key is encrypted at rest (argon2id +
AES\-256\-GCM); every signing operation (\fBcertificate issue\fR,
\fBcertificate revoke\fR) requires
the operator passphrase, used in memory and never persisted.
\fBrotate\-ca\fR re\-encrypts it under a new passphrase without reissuing
anything.
.PP
Every operator secret \(en the CA passphrase, admin/user passwords \(en
must be at least 8 characters and mix at least 2 of upper/lower/digit/
symbol, enforced the same way whether set interactively, via
\fBOVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE\fR / \fBOVCP_USER_PASSWORD\fR, or through the web UI.
\fBOVCP_YOLO_PASSWORD\fR (set to anything non\-empty) waives this
requirement across the CLI and web UI, since \fBserve\fR inherits its
process environment \(en named for what it is, not a setting to reach for
by accident. Every acceptance through this door is still audited
(\fBweak_password_accepted\fR).
.PP
Client private keys are embedded in the exported profile once and never
stored server\-side (no escrow): a lost profile means revoke + reissue, not
recovery. Certificates (public) are kept and downloadable anytime. Optionally
protect a profile's embedded key with its own password
(\fB\-key\-pass\fR / the web UI's field); OpenVPN prompts for it on connect.
.PP
The management socket is unix\-only, never TCP. The admin UI is HTTPS\-only
(self\-signed certificate, regenerated automatically if it expires or the
CN changes), loopback by default, with signed sessions (HttpOnly, Secure,
SameSite=Strict cookies), CSRF tokens on every mutating request, RBAC
(\fBadmin\fR / \fBoperator\fR / \fBreadonly\fR, see \fBuser\fR), optional
TOTP 2FA, rate\-limited logins, and a full audit log (\fBaudit\fR). Failed
logins lock out a username+IP pair after 5 attempts within 15 minutes,
self\-clearing once the window passes; a separate, looser limit locks out
the username alone after 20 attempts in the same window, to catch a brute
force spread across many source IPs.
.PP
\fBvpn config set \-extra\fR / \fBvpn config edit\fR (server config) and
\fBcertificate export\fR's \fB\-custom\-opts\fR (one client's profile) both
append raw openvpn directives verbatim, with no parsing or validation.
Neither needs a role beyond what already gates that surface: server config
is \fBadmin\fR\-only already (\fBPUT /api/vpn/config\fR, same gate \fBvpn
config get|set|preview|edit\fR go through), and whichever role can export a
profile at all already chooses its remote host, port, proto, and cipher. A
bad directive just fails openvpn's next start (server) or that one
client's next connection attempt (client) \(en never other clients.
.PP
The Telegram bot token (see \fBtelegram\fR) is the one secret stored in
plaintext, not hashed or encrypted \(en unlike the CA passphrase, \fBovcp\fR
needs the raw token on every restart to call Telegram's API, so it can't
be argon2id\-hashed the way a password is. Same exposure tier as
\fBOVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE\fR/\fBOVCP_USER_PASSWORD\fR: a leaked token lets
someone send/read messages as that bot, nothing more \(en it grants no
access to the CA, the database, or the admin UI. Every inbound message is
checked against exactly one configured Telegram identity (numeric id or
\fB@username\fR); every other sender gets a 403 reply and the message is
otherwise ignored \(en for the first 3 attempts. Past that, the sender is
blocked for the rest of the process's life: no more 403s, no API call, no
log line. The bot's own command surface is deliberately narrow
(\fB/status\fR reports openvpn and dnsmasq state plus a cert\-expiry
summary; \fB/menu\fR hands out openvpn's Start/Stop/Restart/Reconnect;
\fB/dns\fR hands out dnsmasq's own Start/Stop/Restart \(en Stop and
Restart always behind a confirm step, for both) \(en revoke, user
management, CA rotation, config, and backup are unreachable from Telegram
at any privilege. Its buttons call the same openvpn/dnsmasq lifecycle
methods
.B vpn
and
.B dns
themselves use, so mashing Restart in the chat hits the same overlap guard
described there \(en no more disruptive than doing it from the CLI.
.SH QUICK START
.nf
.RS
# 1. one\-shot setup: CA, server cert, tls\-crypt key, config, admin user
ovcp init \-server\-cn vpn.example.com

# 2. run (admin UI on https://127.0.0.1:8443)
ovcp serve

# 3. hand out a client profile
ovcp certificate export \-cn alice \-remote vpn.example.com > alice.ovpn
.RE
.fi
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init \fB\-server\-cn\fR \fIHOST\fR [\fB\-ca\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-ca\-years\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-server\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-admin\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-mgmt\fR \fIPATH\fR]
One\-shot initialization. Creates the CA (prompting twice for a new
passphrase), issues the server certificate for \fIHOST\fR, generates the
tls\-crypt key, writes \fIserver.conf\fR, and creates the initial admin
user. Idempotent: existing artifacts are kept. Pass \fB\-admin\fR "" to
skip user creation.
.TP
.B init \fB\-skeleton\fR
Creates or repairs \fI$OVCP_DATA\fR's directory tree and permissions only,
skipping the CA/admin/server\-cert steps above entirely \(en safe to run
anytime, and what packaging scripts use instead of their own
\fBmkdir\fR/\fBchown\fR/\fBchmod\fR (see \fBPRIVILEGES\fR).
.TP
.B serve [\fB\-listen\fR \fIADDR\fR] [\fB\-mgmt\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Run the HTTPS admin UI and REST API (default \fI127.0.0.1:8443\fR, self\-signed
certificate generated on first run). \fB\-mgmt\fR (default
\fI/run/ovcp/mgmt.sock\fR, same as \fB$OVCP_MGMT_SOCK\fR) and \fB\-ctrl\fR
(default \fI/run/ovcp/control.sock\fR, same as \fB$OVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR) are two
independent sockets \(en the flag overrides take effect for this
\fBserve\fR only, no env vars required; see \fBOVCP_MGMT_SOCK\fR /
\fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR below for what each is for. Starts the
.B openvpn
worker from \fIserver.conf\fR as a foreground child (reaped on exit \(en no
zombies) with its log in \fIlogs/openvpn.log\fR, and stops it on clean shutdown.
An exit that
.B ovcp
did not itself request (crash, OOM\-kill, an external \fBkill \-9\fR) is
logged at error level and auto\-restarted after 3 seconds; a config bad
enough to make openvpn exit repeatedly trips a breaker after 5 attempts
within 60 seconds, logging an error instead of retrying forever (fix the
config, then \fBovcp vpn start\fR). A deliberate
.B ovcp vpn stop
never auto\-restarts.
Exposes a root\-only control socket so a separate
.B ovcp vpn
invocation can drive the worker. Refuses to start with an
actionable error if
.B ovcp init
has not been run.
.TP
.B certificate issue \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-kind\fR client|server] [\fB\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-out\fR \fIPREFIX\fR] [\fB\-key\-pass\fR \fIPW\fR]
\fBcert\fR is accepted everywhere as a shorthand for \fBcertificate\fR
(\fBovcp cert list\fR, \fBovcp cert export ...\fR); the rest of this page
spells it out in full. Issue a certificate. Client private keys are printed (or written with
\fB\-out\fR) exactly once and never stored; if lost, revoke and reissue.
Certificates (public) are kept and can be re\-downloaded anytime with
\fBcertificate list\fR / the web UI. With \fB\-key\-pass\fR the private key
is AES\-256 encrypted; OpenVPN prompts for the password on connect \(en
client certs only, rejected for \fB\-kind\fR \fIserver\fR (openvpn cannot
unlock an encrypted key non\-interactively at startup). \fB\-days\fR must
be positive. \fB\-cn\fR is capped at 64 bytes (RFC 5280's CommonName
limit). Only one certificate per CN may be active (non\-revoked) at a
time \(en \fBcertificate revoke\fR the existing one first to reissue under
the same CN.
.TP
.B certificate list [\fB\-status\fR all|active|revoked] [\fB\-kind\fR all|client|server] [\fB\-sort\fR cn|kind|expiry|serial] [\fB\-desc\fR]
List issued certificates with status (valid, expiring, REVOKED, expired;
expiring is anything within 30 days of \fBNotAfter\fR, matching the web
UI's Certs tab), colored accordingly (see \fB\-no\-color\fR).
\fB\-status\fR/\fB\-kind\fR filter before
printing (default \fBall\fR, today's behavior). \fB\-sort\fR reorders the
filtered list (default: issued order); \fB\-desc\fR reverses it. Same
filters/sort as the web UI's Certs tab. \fB\-json\fR prints the same rows
as JSON instead (see \fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR).
.TP
.B certificate revoke \fB\-serial\fR \fIHEX\fR
Revoke a certificate and regenerate the CRL. OpenVPN rejects the client
on its next handshake (\fBcrl\-verify\fR is always configured).
.TP
.B certificate export \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-remote\fR \fIHOST\fR] [\fB\-port\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-proto\fR udp|tcp] [\fB\-server\-cn\fR \fICN\fR] [\fB\-out\fR \fIPREFIX\fR] [\fB\-key\-pass\fR \fIPW\fR] [\fB\-split\-tunnel\fR] [\fB\-custom\-opts\fR \fIOPTS\fR|\fB\-\fR]
Issue a fresh client certificate and print a complete inline
\fI.ovpn\fR profile (CA, cert, key, tls\-crypt) on stdout, or write it to
\fIPREFIX\fR\fI.ovpn\fR with \fB\-out\fR (same convention as
\fBcertificate issue\fR). \fB\-remote\fR defaults to \fBOVCP_SERVER_CN\fR /
the server cert's CN, and is only required if neither is set. \fB\-port\fR,
\fB\-proto\fR, and the data cipher default to the server's configured
values (see \fBvpn config get\fR / \fBvpn config set\fR, or the web UI's
Settings tab \(en both stay in sync); pass \fB\-port\fR or \fB\-proto\fR to override
just this one export. \fB\-key\-pass\fR password\-protects the embedded
key. Same one\-active\-cert\-per\-CN rule and 64\-byte \fB\-cn\fR cap as
\fBcertificate issue\fR.
.RS
.TP
\fB\-split\-tunnel\fR
Adds a client\-side \fBpull\-filter\fR that ignores the server's pushed
\fBredirect\-gateway\fR, so this one client keeps its own default route
instead of tunneling all traffic; every other pushed option (DNS, explicit
routes) still applies. Only accepted when the server itself redirects all
traffic (\fBvpn config set \-redirect\-gw\fR / web UI Settings) \(en rejected
otherwise, since there is nothing to opt out of. Also available from the
web UI (Certs tab), shown only when the server redirects.
.TP
\fB\-custom\-opts\fR
Appends raw openvpn directives to this one client's profile, unvalidated
\(en a bad line only breaks that one client's connection, never the
server. Three equivalent forms, pick whichever fits:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
a comma\-separated list, split into one directive per line:
\fB\-custom\-opts\fR "keepalive 5 30,verb 4"
.IP \(bu 2
a bare \fB\-\fR, which opens \fB$EDITOR\fR (falls back to \fBvi\fR) for
multi\-line editing, same idiom as \fBgit commit\fR
.IP \(bu 2
a file's contents by way of the shell:
\fB\-custom\-opts\fR "$(cat FILE)" \(en already newline\-separated with no
commas, so it passes through the comma\-split unchanged
.RE
Also available from the web UI (Certs tab), a plain multi\-line field.
.RE
.TP
.B certificate renew\-server [\fB\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-server\-cn\fR \fICN\fR]
Reissue the openvpn server certificate (a fresh keypair and serial),
revoking the outgoing one and writing the new one to the same paths
.B serve
reads. Defaults to the existing server cert's CN (or \fBOVCP_SERVER_CN\fR);
pass \fB\-server\-cn\fR explicitly right after a \fBbackup restore\fR, which
never restores a server cert (see below). Also available from the web UI
(Settings, admin role). Takes effect on the next
.B ovcp vpn restart
\(en openvpn does not hot\-reload its certificate.
.TP
.B vpn config get
Print the persisted server configuration \(en proto, port, subnet,
cipher, pushed DNS/routes, push\-internal\-dns, redirect\-gateway, and the
raw extra directives \(en the same values \fBGET /api/vpn/config\fR (web UI
Settings tab) reads.
.TP
.B vpn config set [\fB\-proto\fR udp|tcp] [\fB\-port\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-subnet\fR \fICIDR\fR] [\fB\-cipher\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-dns\fR \fIIPS\fR] [\fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR on|off] [\fB\-routes\fR \fICIDRS\fR] [\fB\-redirect\-gw\fR on|off] [\fB\-extra\fR \fIOPTS\fR|\fB\-\fR]
Change one or more server settings and persist them \(en the CLI/env\-var
equivalent of the web UI's Settings tab \fBPUT /api/vpn/config\fR, for
containers and non\-attended initialization where there is no browser to
click Save. Every flag defaults to its zero value, meaning \(lqleave this
setting unchanged\(rq: only the flags actually passed are applied, on top
of whatever is currently persisted (or the built\-in/env\-seeded defaults,
see \fBinit\fR and \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_PROTO\fR and friends below, if nothing
has been saved yet). \fB\-dns\fR and \fB\-routes\fR take a comma\-separated
list (\fB\-dns\fR "1.1.1.1, 9.9.9.9"), replacing the whole list, not
appending to it. \fB\-extra\fR takes the same three forms as
\fBcertificate export\fR's \fB\-custom\-opts\fR (comma list, \fB$(cat
FILE)\fR, or a bare \fB\-\fR for \fB$EDITOR\fR) \(en equivalent to
\fBvpn config edit\fR below, just scriptable inline. \fB\-redirect\-gw\fR and
\fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR accept \fBon\fR/\fBoff\fR, \fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR,
or \fB1\fR/\fB0\fR, case\-insensitive \(en the same boolean vocabulary as
\fBdns config set \-block\-internet\fR below. Rejects an invalid combination
(bad CIDR, disallowed cipher, port out of range, an unrecognized boolean
value) without writing anything. Takes effect on the next
.B ovcp vpn restart
\(en openvpn does not hot\-reload its configuration.
.RS
.TP
\fBPush internal DNS\fR
\fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR (off by default; \fBovcp init\fR turns it on
automatically when it finds dnsmasq on \fBPATH\fR) pushes the server's own
dnsmasq resolver \(en its VPN\-side tun IP, e.g. \fI10.8.0.1\fR \(en to
clients first, ahead of anything in \fB\-dns\fR. That ordering is what lets
internal cert\-hostnames (see \fBrecord\fR below, \fIcerts.hosts\fR /
\fIcustom.hosts\fR) resolve at all: without it, a client only ever asks
whatever DNS it already had, and never learns those names exist. It
interacts directly with \fBdns config set \-block\-internet\fR below: that
setting makes dnsmasq itself refuse to forward upstream, but it's no
protection at all for a client that was never told to use dnsmasq as its
resolver in the first place \(en \fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR is what makes
that client send queries there. Likewise, manual entries left in
\fB\-dns\fR alongside \fB\-block\-internet on\fR let a client route around
the block by falling through to one of those other pushed resolvers.
\fBvpn config set\fR and \fBdns config set\fR both print a non\-fatal
warning (CLI: stderr; web UI: inline) when they detect either combination,
but never block the save \(en it's occasionally the intended setup (e.g. a
resolver deliberately kept reachable for split\-DNS).
.RE
.TP
.B vpn config preview [\fIsame flags as\fR \fBvpn config set\fR]
Show what \fBvpn config set\fR with the same flags would change in
\fIserver.conf\fR (unified \fB+\fR/\fB\-\fR diff lines), without persisting
or writing anything \(en the CLI twin of the web UI's \fBPreview\fR
button (\fBPOST /api/vpn/config/preview\fR). Safe to run anytime: nothing is
saved until the same flags are run through \fBvpn config set\fR instead \(en
\fBvpn config get\fR right after a \fBpreview\fR still shows the old values.
.TP
.B vpn config edit
Edit the server config's raw extra directives in \fB$EDITOR\fR (falls
back to \fBvi\fR), same idiom as \fBgit commit\fR / \fBcrontab \-e\fR.
Saved text is appended verbatim to the next rendered \fIserver.conf\fR,
unvalidated \(en a bad line fails on the next restart. Equivalent to
\fBvpn config set \-extra \-\fR. Also available from the web UI (Settings,
admin role, a plain multi\-line field); all three write the same
persisted value. Takes effect on the next
.B ovcp vpn restart
\(en openvpn does not hot\-reload its configuration.
.TP
.B rotate\-ca
Re\-encrypt the CA private key under a new passphrase (prompts for the
current one, then the new one twice). The CA keypair and certificate are
unchanged \(en issued certificates stay valid, nothing needs reissuing.
\fICLI\-only\fR by design \(en a rare maintenance operation, not a routine
one like \fBcertificate issue\fR/\fBcertificate revoke\fR, so the new
passphrase never has to cross the HTTP surface at all.
.TP
.B backup create [\fB\-out\fR \fIFILE\fR]
Write an encrypted archive (argon2id + AES\-256\-GCM, prompts twice for a
backup passphrase) of the CA, CRL, tls\-crypt key, TOTP key,
\fIserver.conf\fR, and database. Also available from the web UI (Settings, admin role).
Deliberately excludes the openvpn server certificate and private key:
OpenVPN clients trust the CA chain and a CN match, never a pinned server
cert, so a restore just issues a fresh one (\fBcertificate
renew\-server\fR) \(en the server's private key never has to leave the
machine. Client private keys
were never stored here to begin with (no escrow); nothing to back up there
either.
.TP
.B backup restore [\fB\-force\fR] \fIFILE\fR
Decrypt and extract a backup into \fB\-data\fR (must be an uninitialized
directory, or pass \fB\-force\fR to overwrite one). \fICLI\-only\fR by
design \(en restoring is an offline, disaster\-recovery operation against a
data directory, not something to expose over HTTP on a possibly\-live
server. Follow with \fBcertificate renew\-server\fR (the archive has no
server cert) and \fBovcp vpn start\fR.
.TP
.B status [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Overview: whether the openvpn worker is running (with its pid) and, if so,
the connected clients \(en both via the control socket, see
\fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR. Traffic history and per\-client sessions are
\fBstats\fR's job, not this command's.
.TP
.B stats [\fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR] [\fB\-follow\fR] [\fB\-interval\fR|\fB\-int\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.TQ
.B stats config get
.TQ
.B stats config set [\fB\-sample\-interval\-sec\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-retention\-hours\fR \fIN\fR]
Traffic history: recent throughput/volume and finished client sessions,
globally or for one client (\fB\-cn\fR), read straight from the database
\(en works even with \fBserve\fR not running. Same data as the web UI's
Stats tab. \fB\-follow\fR switches to a live top\-like view instead: polls
a running \fBserve\fR's control socket (not the database) every
\fB\-interval\fR (alias \fB\-int\fR) seconds (default 2) and redraws in
place, sorted busiest\-first when global; rejects \fB\-json\fR (a live
view, not a single result) and needs \fBserve\fR running.
.B stats config get\fR/\fBstats config set
manage the sampler that feeds all of the above, independently of
\fB\-follow\fR/\fB\-interval\fR (which only control this one invocation's
polling, not the sampler itself): \fB\-sample\-interval\-sec\fR (default
60, seeded from \fBOVCP_STATS_SAMPLE_INTERVAL\fR) is how often a sample is
written per connected client; \fB\-retention\-hours\fR (default 24, seeded
from \fBOVCP_STATS_RETENTION_HOURS\fR) is how far back samples/sessions
are kept and queried, by this command and \fBGET /api/stats\fR alike. Both
must be positive. Same persisted values as \fBGET\fR/\fBPUT
/api/stats/config\fR (web UI); picked up by the running sampler on its
next tick, no restart needed.
.TP
.B vpn start|stop|restart|reconnect|status [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
.TQ
.B vpn kill \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Manage or inspect the openvpn worker owned by a running
.B serve
(over the control socket, see \fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR).
.B start
launches it (idempotent),
.B stop
sends SIGTERM,
.B status
reports whether it is running,
.B restart
is a full stop + fresh start \(en required for any configuration, key, or
CRL change \(en and
.B reconnect
sends SIGUSR1 for a soft session reset (runtime recovery, no process
replacement).
.B kill
disconnects one connected client immediately by CN, without touching the
worker process itself. SIGHUP / in\-place reload is intentionally not
supported: a fresh restart re\-reads every root\-owned file, which SIGHUP
cannot. Each lifecycle op prints the resulting openvpn pid, so a repeated
.B start
reports \(lqalready started\(rq rather than spawning a second process.
.BR start / stop / restart / reconnect
reject instead of queuing behind one already in flight for the same
worker (\(lqoperation already in progress\(rq) \(en a second call has to
wait for the first to actually finish, not just get delayed behind it;
a scripted loop calling
.B restart
faster than openvpn itself restarts gets mostly rejections, not a stack
of real restarts. \fBdns\fR's lifecycle ops and \fBtelegram\fR's
\fBstart\fR/\fBstop\fR/\fBrestart\fR share the same guard, independently
per worker.
.TP
.B dns start|stop|restart|status [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Manage or inspect the dnsmasq worker the same way
.B vpn
manages openvpn. No
.B reconnect
(dnsmasq has no such signal). If dnsmasq isn't installed, every op
fails with a clear error.
.TP
.B dns config get
.TQ
.B dns config set [\fB\-block\-internet\fR on|off]
Whether dnsmasq also forwards to upstream resolvers, or answers only
names it knows locally \(en the same persisted setting as the Records
tab's \(lqBlock internet DNS\(rq toggle
(\fBGET\fR/\fBPUT /api/dns/config\fR). Unlike
.BR start / stop / restart / status
above, this is a stored setting, not a live control\-socket op \(en it
works without a running
.B serve
and takes effect on dnsmasq's next
.B start
or
.BR restart .
Default off, seeded from \fBOVCP_DNS_BLOCK_INTERNET\fR (\fBtrue\fR turns it
on) only until the first save \(en see \fBvpn config set\fR above for the
same seed\-once\-then\-persisted pattern.
.B dns config get
prints the current value; \fB\-block\-internet\fR on \fBdns config set\fR
takes the same boolean vocabulary as \fBvpn config set \-redirect\-gw\fR (\fBon\fR/
\fBoff\fR, \fBtrue\fR/\fBfalse\fR, \fB1\fR/\fB0\fR, case\-insensitive);
omitting it makes \fBset\fR a no\-op. See \fBPush internal DNS\fR under
\fBvpn config set\fR above for how this interacts with that setting:
turning this on without also pushing the internal resolver, or combining
it with manually pushed DNS servers (\fBvpn config set \-dns\fR), leaves a
documented leak that both commands warn about on save (non\-fatal).
.TP
.B record list [\fB\-status\fR all|active|revoked] [\fB\-kind\fR all|client|server] [\fB\-sort\fR cn|kind|expiry|serial] [\fB\-desc\fR]
.TQ
.B record edit
.TQ
.B record include|exclude \fB\-cn\fR \fINAME\fR
Manage internal DNS records (see \fBFILES\fR, \fIcerts.hosts\fR and
\fIcustom.hosts\fR).
.B list
prints every certificate (not just currently-connected ones) with its DNS
include/exclude status, same filters/sort/coloring as \fBcertificate
list\fR, plus \fIcustom.hosts\fR' raw content.
.B edit
opens the custom entries in \fB$EDITOR\fR (fallback vi), same as
\fBvpn config edit\fR.
.B include
and
.B exclude
control whether a certificate resolves while connected (default:
included), mirroring \fBuser enable|disable\fR.
.TP
.B logs config get
.TQ
.B logs config set [\fB\-enabled\fR on|off] [\fB\-retention\-days\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-keep\-uncompressed\fR \fIN\fR]
.TQ
.B logs rotate
.TQ
.B logs status
Manage rotation of \fIopenvpn.log\fR/\fIovcp.log\fR/\fIdnsmasq.log\fR (see
\fBFILES\fR): internal copytruncate, safe with the worker's already\-open
handle, no reopen or restart needed. Runs once a day by default while
\fBserve\fR is up. Same persisted setting as \fBGET\fR/\fBPUT
/api/logs/config\fR (\fBPOST /api/logs/rotate\fR for \fBrotate\fR).
.B logs config set \-enabled\fR
(default on, seeded from \fBOVCP_LOG_ROTATE_ENABLED\fR \(en anything but
\fBfalse\fR means on) toggles that daily pass \(en turn it off to hand
rotation to an external \fBlogrotate\fR instead (see \fI/var/log/ovcp\fR in
\fBFILES\fR).
.B \-retention\-days
(default 14, seeded from \fBOVCP_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS\fR; \fB0\fR keeps
forever) deletes rotated generations older than that;
.B \-keep\-uncompressed
(default 2, seeded from \fBOVCP_LOG_KEEP_UNCOMPRESSED\fR) gzips anything
older than the newest N. Like \fBvpn config\fR's and \fBdns config\fR's env
vars, these only seed the first, never\-yet\-persisted default \(en once
saved, \fBlogs config set\fR is the way to change it, not the environment.
.B rotate
runs one pass immediately \(en point an external cron at this if
\fB\-enabled\fR is off \(en and works whether or not \fBserve\fR is
running, same as \fBrecord\fR's CLI\-side refresh.
.B status
prints each source's current size, generation count, and oldest/newest
rotation timestamp.
.TP
.B user add \fB\-name\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-role\fR admin|operator|readonly]
.TQ
.B user list [\fB\-sort\fR username|role|created] [\fB\-desc\fR]
.TQ
.B user del|disable|enable|passwd \fB\-name\fR \fINAME\fR
.TQ
.B user totp \fB\-name\fR \fINAME\fR [\fB\-off\fR]
Manage web UI users. Roles: \fBadmin\fR (everything), \fBoperator\fR
(certificates, disconnects, default for \fBadd\fR), \fBreadonly\fR.
\fBuser list\fR's \fB\-sort\fR reorders (default: today's order); \fB\-desc\fR
reverses it, and its enabled/DISABLED status is colored (see
\fB\-no\-color\fR). Same as the web UI's Users tab \(en see
\fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR for \fB\-json\fR's fields per subcommand.
.B user totp
prints an otpauth:// enrollment URL; \fB\-off\fR disables 2FA instead.
\fBuser del\fR and \fBuser disable\fR have no self\-lockout guard on the
CLI \(en unlike the REST API, which refuses to delete or disable the
caller's own account. Take care when scripting either against the
currently\-authenticated admin.
.TP
.B audit
Print the last 50 audit entries (logins, issues, revocations, kills,
config changes), newest first.
.TP
.B debug on|off [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Toggle verbose logging on a running \fBserve\fR (over the control socket,
see \fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR) \(en no restart, resets to normal on the next one.
.TP
.B telegram config get
.TQ
.B telegram config set [\fB\-token\fR] [\fB\-admin\fR \fIID\-or\-@user\fR]
.TQ
.B telegram start|stop|restart|status [\fB\-ctrl\fR \fIPATH\fR]
Notify\-only Telegram bot, with a narrow control surface. \fBtelegram
config set\fR sets the bot token and the one Telegram identity the bot
will ever respond to independently \(en pass either or both, at least one
required. \fB\-token\fR is a boolean flag, not a value (a secret has no
business on the command line): prompts interactively, or reads
\fBOVCP_TELEGRAM_TOKEN\fR, validated against Telegram's \fBgetMe\fR before
saving. \fB\-admin\fR takes the numeric user id or \fB@username\fR
directly (default \fBOVCP_TELEGRAM_ADMIN\fR if set); anyone else who
messages the bot gets a 403 reply and nothing else.
\fBtelegram config get\fR prints whether a token is set, the current admin,
and whether the chat is linked yet (a \fB@username\fR admin only links once
they've messaged the bot; re\-saving the same token or admin never unlinks
it) \(en never the token itself \(en same values as \fBGET\fR/\fBPUT
/api/telegram/config\fR (web UI). Changing \fB\-admin\fR to a different
identity best\-effort warns the chat being replaced first, before it's
unlinked \(en the one defense against a hijacked reassignment; changing
\fB\-token\fR has no equivalent, an already\-leaked token grants that access
regardless.
\fBstart\fR/\fBstop\fR/\fBrestart\fR/\fBstatus\fR drive the poller inside a
running \fBserve\fR over the control socket (see \fBOVCP_CTRL_SOCK\fR),
same as \fBvpn\fR; the poller also auto\-starts with \fBserve\fR if a token
is already set. Once linked, the bot pushes notifications for revoke,
user add/del, CA rotate, a cert (client, server, or the CA itself) entering
its 30\-day expiry window (once per cert; also logged at Warn in
\fIovcp.log\fR regardless of Telegram config), and openvpn/dnsmasq giving up
auto\-restart after crash\-looping;
\fB/status\fR, \fB/menu\fR (openvpn Start/Stop/Restart/Reconnect), and
\fB/dns\fR (dnsmasq Start/Stop/Restart, same confirm step on Stop/Restart)
are the only commands it accepts \(en revoke, user management, CA
rotation, config, and backup stay CLI/web\-login only.
.TP
.B version
Print \fBovcp\fR's version, and the \fBopenvpn\fR and \fBdnsmasq\fR binaries'
versions and paths if found on \fBPATH\fR (advisory only \(en absent, not an
error, if not). Without dnsmasq, \fBdns\fR/\fBrecord\fR still run but fail
cleanly, and the web UI hides the Records tab.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Print a shell completion script to stdout. Install once, e.g.
\fBovcp completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/ovcp\fR; distro packages
already install these (see \fBBUILD.md\fR). Rejects \fB\-json\fR (see
\fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-data \fIDIR\fR
Data directory. Overrides \fB$OVCP_DATA\fR for this invocation; if neither
is given, defaults to \fI/var/lib/ovcp\fR. This directory is the root every
path in \fBFILES\fR below is relative to. Must come before the command
(\fBovcp \-data\fR \fIDIR\fR \fICOMMAND\fR), like \fBgit \-C\fR / \fBdocker
\-H\fR.
.TP
.B \-no\-color
Disable ANSI color: the status words in \fBcertificate list\fR/\fBstatus\fR/
\fBuser list\fR's text output, and the level tag in \fBovcp\fR's own log lines.
Same effect as \fB$NO_COLOR\fR. Color is already off automatically when the
relevant stream (stdout for table output, stderr for logs) isn't a
terminal \(en piped, journal, redirected to a file. Must come before the
command (\fBovcp \-no\-color\fR \fICOMMAND\fR), like \fB\-data\fR.
.TP
.B \-json
Machine\-readable JSON instead of human text, for whatever the command
produces. Same as \fB$OVCP_JSON\fR. For every command except \fBserve\fR,
this means its result on stdout (see \fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR
below). For \fBserve\fR, which
has no single result, it means \fBovcp\fR's own log lines \(en both the
console/journal stream and the tee'd \fI$OVCP_DATA/logs/ovcp.log\fR \(en as
one JSON object per line (\fBtime\fR, \fBlevel\fR, \fBmsg\fR, plus whatever
attributes that log call carried) instead of \fBslog\fR's default
\fBkey=value\fR text, for log shippers/parsers. The web UI's Logs tab parses
either format. Must come before the command, like \fB\-data\fR (\fBovcp
\-json list\fR, \fBovcp \-json serve\fR).
.TP
.B \-debug
Debug\-level logging for this invocation only \(en every \fBslog.Debug\fR
call (HTTP request logging, telegram polling, ...) starts showing up
immediately, no restart or second command needed. For a running
\fBserve\fR, equivalent to sending \fBdebug on\fR over the control socket at
startup instead of afterwards (see \fBdebug\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR). Must come
before the command, like \fB\-data\fR.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print the command list (\fBCOMMANDS\fR above, condensed) and exit.
.SH MACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT
\fB\-json\fR (or \fB$OVCP_JSON\fR) switches every command \(en not a
subset \(en from aligned human text to JSON on stdout. It changes only the
wire format: the same fields are computed either way, so nothing in text is
missing from JSON or vice versa, and the two modes are otherwise
interchangeable in scripts and by hand. Two exceptions, both because there
is no single result to encode: \fBstats \-follow\fR (a live redrawing view,
rejects \fB\-json\fR outright) and \fBcompletion\fR (a shell script, also
rejected \(en piping it through JSON would break \fBeval "$(ovcp completion
bash)"\fR, its whole point). \fBserve\fR is the other special case: it never
exits with a single result, so \fB\-json\fR there means JSON log lines
instead (see \fB\-json\fR in \fBOPTIONS\fR).
.PP
Fatal errors are JSON too, on stdout, as \fB{"error": "..."}\fR \(en the
same shape every failed HTTP request in the REST API already uses. A script
can parse stdout unconditionally and check \fB.error\fR (or the process exit
code, still 1 on failure) rather than branching on success vs. failure
output. Flag\-parsing mistakes (an unknown or malformed flag) are the one
exception, always plain text on stderr from Go's own flag parser, before
\fBovcp\fR ever sees \fB\-json\fR.
.PP
\fB\-json\fR is a global flag: it comes before the command, like
\fB\-data\fR/\fB\-debug\fR/\fB\-no\-color\fR (\fBovcp \-json certificate
list\fR, not \fBovcp certificate list \-json\fR).
.PP
Fully unattended (Ansible, CI, any tool with no terminal to answer a
prompt): every passphrase/password/token \fBovcp\fR ever asks for also
reads from an env var (\fBOVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE\fR and friends, see
\fBENVIRONMENT\fR) \(en set it and the prompt never fires, on any command,
in either output mode. Combine with \fB\-json\fR/\fB$OVCP_JSON\fR for a
clean, scriptable round trip:
.RS
.nf
OVCP_JSON=1 OVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE=\(dq...\(dq ovcp certificate issue \-cn alice
.fi
.RE
No env var and no terminal (stdin not a tty) fails fast with a JSON
\fB{"error": "..."}\fR instead of hanging on a prompt nobody can answer.
Per\-command JSON shapes:
.TP
.B init
\fB{caCert, serverCert, tlsCrypt, serverConf, admin, adminUIURL}\fR. The
\fB[N/5]\fR progress lines are human text only, suppressed under
\fB\-json\fR so they can't interleave with the result.
.TP
.B certificate issue, certificate export
\fB{serial, cert, key}\fR or \fB{serial, certFile, keyFile}\fR with
\fB\-out\fR \(en the cert/key (or \fI.ovpn\fR bundle, \fBbundle\fR not
\fBcert\fR/\fBkey\fR, for \fBexport\fR) inline as JSON strings instead of
raw bytes on stdout, or the paths they were written to.
.TP
.B certificate revoke
\fB{serial, crlPath}\fR.
.TP
.B rotate\-ca, vpn config edit, backup restore, user del,
.B user passwd, user totp \-off, record edit|include|exclude, init \-skeleton,
.B logs rotate
\fB{message}\fR \(en a confirmation string, nothing structured beyond it.
.TP
.B certificate renew\-server
\fB{serverCN, serial}\fR.
.TP
.B backup create
\fB{file}\fR.
.TP
.B certificate list
array of \fB{status, kind, cn, serial, expires}\fR.
.TP
.B vpn config get, vpn config set
The full persisted server config: \fB{Proto, Port, Subnet, Cipher, DNS,
PushInternalDNS, Routes, RedirectGW, Extra}\fR plus the \fBovcp\fR\-managed
path/runtime fields (\fBCACert, ServerCert, ServerKey, CRL, TLSCrypt,
MgmtSocket, StatusLog, StatusInterval, RunAsUser, RunAsGroup, ServerCN\fR).
Deliberately \fInot\fR this page's usual lowercase field\-name convention
\(en it's the same untagged Go struct \fBGET\fR/\fBPUT /api/vpn/config\fR
already return, so a script gets byte\-identical shapes from the CLI and
the web UI for this one resource.
.TP
.B vpn config preview
\fB{diff, changed}\fR: \fBdiff\fR an array of unified \fB+\fR/\fB\-\fR
\fIserver.conf\fR lines (empty if \fBchanged\fR is \fBfalse\fR).
.TP
.B dns config get, dns config set
\fB{BlockInternet}\fR \(en same untagged\-struct exception as \fBvpn
config\fR above, for the CLI's own JSON. \fINote:\fR the REST API's
\fBGET\fR/\fBPUT /api/dns/config\fR instead wrap the same value as
\fB{"blockInternet": ...}\fR, lowercase \(en unlike \fBvpn config\fR, this
is the one settings resource where the CLI and the web API don't share a
byte\-identical shape.
.TP
.B logs config get, logs config set
\fB{Enabled, RetentionDays, KeepUncompressed}\fR, same untagged\-struct
convention as \fBvpn config\fR above \(en \fBGET\fR/\fBPUT
/api/logs/config\fR return the identical shape.
.TP
.B logs status
array of \fB{Name, Path, CurrentSize, Generations, Oldest, Newest}\fR, one
row per log source (see \fBlogs\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR).
.TP
.B stats config get, stats config set
\fB{SampleIntervalSec, RetentionHours}\fR, same untagged\-struct convention
as \fBvpn config\fR above \(en \fBGET\fR/\fBPUT /api/stats/config\fR return
the identical shape.
.TP
.B status
\fB{running, pid, uptime, ovcpUptime, error, clients}\fR \(en \fBpid\fR,
\fBuptime\fR, \fBovcpUptime\fR, and \fBerror\fR are omitted, not sent as
empty/zero, when not applicable (e.g. \fBuptime\fR while stopped).
.TP
.B stats
\fB{samples, sessions}\fR (snapshot mode only \(en see \fB\-follow\fR
above).
.TP
.B vpn kill
\fB{message}\fR.
.TP
.B vpn (other ops), dns (excl. config), debug
\fBvpn\fR/\fBdns\fR lifecycle ops (\fBstart|stop|restart|reconnect|status\fR):
\fB{Pid, Changed, StartedAt, ServeStartedAt}\fR (untagged, like \fBvpn
config\fR above) for every op including \fBstatus\fR \(en \fBdns config\fR
is documented separately above. \fBdebug on\fR/\fBoff\fR: \fB{debug}\fR.
.TP
.B record list
\fB{certs, custom}\fR: \fBcerts\fR an array of \fB{status, kind, cn,
serial, expires, includeDNS}\fR (same \fBstatus\fR values as
\fBcertificate list\fR); \fBcustom\fR is \fIcustom.hosts\fR' raw content as
one string.
.TP
.B telegram config get, telegram config set, telegram start|stop|restart|status
\fB{running, tokenSet, admin, chatLinked}\fR \(en same shape from every one
of these, whether or not \fB\-token\fR/\fB\-admin\fR were passed to
\fBconfig set\fR.
.TP
.B user add
\fB{username, role}\fR.
.TP
.B user list
array of \fB{Username, Role, Disabled, TOTP, CreatedAt}\fR \(en never the
password hash or TOTP secret, in JSON any more than in text.
.TP
.B user disable|enable
\fB{username, disabled}\fR.
.TP
.B user totp
(enrollment) \fB{secret, url}\fR \(en the QR code itself is terminal art,
skipped entirely under \fB\-json\fR.
.TP
.B audit
array of the same entries as text.
.TP
.B version
\fB{version, platform, buildTarget, openvpnVersion, openvpnPath,
dnsmasqVersion, dnsmasqPath}\fR \(en each pair omitted if that binary isn't
on \fBPATH\fR (see \fBversion\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OVCP_DATA
Data directory \(en the source of truth for where ovcp keeps everything
(see \fBFILES\fR). Defaults to \fI/var/lib/ovcp\fR if unset; \fB\-data\fR
overrides it for a single invocation.
.TP
.B OVCP_LISTEN
Admin UI listen address(es), comma\-separated. Each gets the full
HTTPS/auth stack. VPN\-side addresses (e.g. 10.8.0.1:8443, reachable by
connected clients) may be bound before the tun interface exists
(IP_FREEBIND).
.TP
.B OVCP_MGMT_SOCK
OpenVPN management socket path (default \fI/run/ovcp/mgmt.sock\fR); same
path \fBserve \-mgmt\fR sets for a single invocation, no env var needed.
Read/written only by \fBserve\fR itself (and \fBinit\fR/\fBserve\fR, when
writing it into server.conf) \(en OpenVPN's management interface serves
exactly one connected client, ever, and \fBserve\fR holds that connection
for its whole life. Nothing else dials this socket directly.
.TP
.B OVCP_CTRL_SOCK
ovcp control socket for \fBvpn\fR (including \fBkill\fR), \fBdns\fR,
\fBtelegram\fR, \fBdebug\fR, and \fBstats \-follow\fR (default
\fI/run/ovcp/control.sock\fR); same path \fBserve \-ctrl\fR sets for a
single invocation, no env var needed. The CLI talks to a running
\fBserve\fR here for anything that needs its in\-process state \(en only
\fBvpn\fR's \fBstatus\fR/\fBkill\fR/\fBreconnect\fR and \fBstats \-follow\fR
actually proxy through to \fBserve\fR's own \fBOVCP_MGMT_SOCK\fR
connection, per above; \fBdns\fR/\fBtelegram\fR lifecycle ops and
\fBdebug\fR answer from \fBserve\fR's own state directly, never touching
the management socket. This is a \fIseparate\fR socket from
\fBOVCP_MGMT_SOCK\fR/\fB\-mgmt\fR above \(en overriding one does not move
the other; pass both flags (or set both env vars) to run \fBserve\fR
entirely out of a non\-root\-writable directory.
.TP
.B OVCP_STATUS_INTERVAL
Seconds between openvpn rewriting \fI/run/ovcp/status\fR (see \fBFILES\fR)
and how often the web UI polls it (default \fB3\fR, clamped to 1\-60).
Invalid values fall back to the default with a logged warning.
.TP
.B OVCP_SERVER_CN
Default client \fBremote\fR for \fBcertificate export\fR and CN for the
admin UI certificate (falls back to the VPN server certificate's CN).
.TP
.B OVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE, OVCP_USER_PASSWORD
Non\-interactive passphrase/password for automation, tests, and container
init (the image defaults both to \fIchangeme!\fR). Avoid on
shared systems: environment variables can leak.
.TP
.B OVCP_CA_NEW_PASSPHRASE, OVCP_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE
Non\-interactive equivalents for \fBrotate\-ca\fR's new passphrase and
\fBbackup\fR's archive passphrase. Same leak caveat as above.
.TP
.B OVCP_YOLO_PASSWORD
Set (to anything non\-empty) to waive the length/complexity check on every
passphrase/password above, CLI and web UI alike. Every weak secret it lets
through is still audited as \fBweak_password_accepted\fR. Named for what it
is, not a setting to reach for by accident.
.TP
.B OVCP_TELEGRAM_TOKEN
Non\-interactive equivalent for \fBtelegram config set \-token\fR's bot
token. Same leak caveat as above; stored in the database afterward
regardless (see \fBSECURITY\fR).
.TP
.B OVCP_TELEGRAM_ADMIN
Non\-interactive equivalent for \fBtelegram config set\fR's \fB\-admin\fR
flag: the numeric Telegram id or \fB@username\fR the bot will ever respond
to.
.TP
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_USER, OVCP_OPENVPN_GROUP
Unprivileged account openvpn drops to after startup (default \fInobody\fR
/ \fInogroup\fR). The container image sets \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_GROUP=nobody\fR
since Alpine has no \fInogroup\fR.
.TP
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_PROTO, OVCP_OPENVPN_PORT, OVCP_OPENVPN_SUBNET,
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_CIPHER, OVCP_OPENVPN_DNS, OVCP_OPENVPN_ROUTES,
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_REDIRECT_GW, OVCP_OPENVPN_EXTRA
Seed every openvpn server setting \fBvpn config get\fR/\fBvpn config set\fR/
the web UI's Settings tab can show or change \(en \fIudp\fR, \fI1194\fR,
\fI10.8.0.0/24\fR, \fIAES\-256\-GCM\fR, unset (no pushed DNS), unset (no
pushed routes), \fItrue\fR (set \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_REDIRECT_GW=false\fR to
disable), and empty by default, respectively. \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_DNS\fR and
\fBOVCP_OPENVPN_ROUTES\fR take a comma\-separated list, same as \fBvpn
config set\fR's \fB\-dns\fR/\fB\-routes\fR flags. There is no env var for
\fB\-push\-internal\-dns\fR \(en it defaults to off, except that \fBinit\fR
itself turns it on when it finds dnsmasq on \fBPATH\fR (see \fBinit\fR and
\fBvpn config set\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR). The one\-shot, non\-attended
path for containers and provisioning tools that need a non\-default
protocol/port/subnet/etc. at \fBinit\fR time without a CLI flag or a
browser: read once by \fBinit\fR (and any first config load before
anything has been explicitly saved) \(en once a config is persisted,
\fBvpn config set\fR is the way to change it, not re\-exporting these and
restarting. See \fBvpn config set\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR for the flag
equivalents and their exact defaults/validation.
.TP
.B OVCP_DNS_BLOCK_INTERNET
Seeds \fBdns config set \-block\-internet\fR's persisted default: set to
\fItrue\fR to start with it on. Same one\-shot\-seed\-then\-persisted
pattern as \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_*\fR above \(en see \fBdns config set\fR in
\fBCOMMANDS\fR.
.TP
.B OVCP_STATS_SAMPLE_INTERVAL, OVCP_STATS_RETENTION_HOURS
Seed \fBstats config set\fR's \fB\-sample\-interval\-sec\fR/
\fB\-retention\-hours\fR (default \fB60\fR seconds, \fB24\fR hours). Same
one\-shot\-seed\-then\-persisted pattern as \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_*\fR above \(en
see \fBstats\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR.
.TP
.B OVCP_LOG_ROTATE_ENABLED, OVCP_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS, OVCP_LOG_KEEP_UNCOMPRESSED
Seed \fBlogs config set\fR's \fB\-enabled\fR/\fB\-retention\-days\fR/
\fB\-keep\-uncompressed\fR (default enabled, \fB14\fR days, \fB2\fR
generations kept uncompressed). \fBOVCP_LOG_ROTATE_ENABLED\fR follows the
same \(lqanything but \fBfalse\fR means on\(rq rule as
\fBOVCP_OPENVPN_REDIRECT_GW\fR; the other two are plain integers. Same
one\-shot\-seed\-then\-persisted pattern as \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_*\fR above \(en
see \fBlogs\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR.
.TP
.B OVCP_DNSMASQ_USER, OVCP_DNSMASQ_GROUP
Same as \fBOVCP_OPENVPN_USER\fR/\fBGROUP\fR, but dnsmasq's own account \(en
independently configurable, same defaults.
.TP
.B OVCP_OPENVPN_AUTOSTART, OVCP_DNSMASQ_AUTOSTART
Set to \fBfalse\fR to skip starting that worker automatically when
\fBserve\fR starts (default: both start). Either can still be started
manually afterward (\fBovcp vpn start\fR / \fBovcp dns start\fR, or the
web UI); this only gates the boot\-time launch.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Same as \fB\-no\-color\fR: disable ANSI color in \fBcertificate list\fR/
\fBstatus\fR/\fBuser list\fR's table output and in \fBovcp\fR's own log
lines. Color is
already off automatically when the relevant stream isn't a terminal
(piped, journal, redirected to a file).
.TP
.B OVCP_JSON
Same as \fB\-json\fR: JSON instead of human text, for every command (see
\fBMACHINE\-READABLE OUTPUT\fR). Set it once in the environment \(en a
container, a CI runner, an automation user's shell profile \(en instead of
passing \fB\-json\fR on every invocation.
.SH FILES
All paths below are rooted at \fB$OVCP_DATA\fR (default \fI/var/lib/ovcp\fR)
\(en set it once and everything follows, including these.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/pki/ca.crt, ca.key.enc
CA certificate and encrypted private key.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/pki/server.crt, server.key
Server keypair used by openvpn. Not included in \fBbackup\fR archives \(en
reissued by \fBcertificate renew\-server\fR after a restore.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/pki/crl.pem
Certificate revocation list (referenced by \fBcrl\-verify\fR).
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/pki/tls\-crypt.key
OpenVPN static key for tls\-crypt (control channel encryption).
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/totp.key
Machine\-local key that decrypts each user's TOTP secret in the database.
Unlike the CA key, not passphrase\-protected \(en ovcp must read it
unattended on every restart. Included in \fBbackup\fR archives.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/server.conf
Generated openvpn configuration. Managed by ovcp; do not edit.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/ovcp.db
SQLite database: users, sessions, certificate metadata, audit log,
connection stats (Stats tab; 24\-hour retention). Private keys are never
stored in the database.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/admin\-tls/
Self\-signed certificate for the admin UI.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/logs/openvpn.log
openvpn's stdout/stderr, appended across restarts. Rotated internally by
default \(en see \fBlogs\fR in \fBCOMMANDS\fR.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/logs/ovcp.log
ovcp's own log, mirrored to stderr/journal. \fBkey=value\fR text by
default, or one JSON object per line with \fB\-json\fR (see \fBOPTIONS\fR).
Rotated the same way as \fIopenvpn.log\fR.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/logs/dnsmasq.log
dnsmasq's stdout/stderr, appended across restarts. Rotated the same way.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/logs/*.log\-\fIYYYYMMDDThhmmssZ\fR[.gz]
Rotated generations (see \fBlogs\fR): plain text for the newest few, gzip
beyond that, deleted past the configured retention.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/dns/certs.hosts
One line per connected, included certificate: server always, clients
while they're online. Regenerated automatically; do not edit.
.TP
.I $OVCP_DATA/dns/custom.hosts
Manually added hosts\-file entries, edited via \fBrecord edit\fR or the
Records tab. Not touched by ovcp otherwise.
.TP
\fBGET /api/logs/download\fR
Not a file, but reads from the ones above: an \fBoperator\fR\-or\-higher
user can fetch a zip of every regular file under \fIlogs/\fR plus the
audit trail and a \fIstatus.json\fR snapshot, unencrypted (unlike
\fBbackup create\fR). Web UI only; \fBreadonly\fR sees the Logs tab but
not this button.
.TP
.I /var/log/ovcp
Symlink to \fI$OVCP_DATA/logs\fR (package/container installs), for a
log\-shipper or an external \fBlogrotate\fR used instead of the internal one.
.TP
.I $OVCP_MGMT_SOCK, $OVCP_CTRL_SOCK
OpenVPN management socket (unix only, never TCP) and ovcp's root\-only
(0600) control socket for \fBvpn\fR/\fBdns\fR/\fBtelegram\fR lifecycle ops,
\fBdebug\fR, and \fBstats \-follow\fR. Default to \fI/run/ovcp/mgmt.sock\fR
and \fI/run/ovcp/control.sock\fR.
.TP
.I /run/ovcp/status
openvpn's periodic connected\-clients snapshot, read by \fBstatus\fR/
\fBstats \-follow\fR/the web UI instead of polling the mgmt socket (which
would log a line per poll). Live state, not a log: excluded from
\fBbackup\fR and from log rotation.
.SH EXAMPLES
Issue and download a profile for a phone, valid two years:
.nf
.RS
ovcp certificate export \-cn dana\-phone \-remote vpn.example.com > dana\-phone.ovpn
.RE
.fi
Revoke it later:
.nf
.RS
ovcp certificate list
ovcp certificate revoke \-serial 682b2da83f4d...
.RE
.fi
Serve the admin UI on the host loopback and again inside the VPN itself, so
it's reachable through the tunnel without ever exposing it publicly (works
even before tun0 exists \(en see \fBIP_FREEBIND\fR under \fBOVCP_LISTEN\fR):
.nf
.RS
OVCP_LISTEN=127.0.0.1:8443,10.8.0.1:8443 ovcp serve
.RE
.fi
A laptop that should reach the VPN subnet but keep its own default route
(the server redirects all traffic for everyone else):
.nf
.RS
ovcp certificate export \-cn dana\-laptop \-remote vpn.example.com \-split\-tunnel > dana\-laptop.ovpn
.RE
.fi
Add a couple of extra directives to one client's profile, and separately
edit the server's own extra directives:
.nf
.RS
ovcp certificate export \-cn dana\-phone \-remote vpn.example.com \e
  \-custom\-opts "block\-outside\-dns,ping\-restart 30" > dana\-phone.ovpn
ovcp vpn config edit
.RE
.fi
Move the server to TCP/443 and stop routing all client traffic through
the VPN, previewing the \fIserver.conf\fR change first:
.nf
.RS
ovcp vpn config preview \-proto tcp \-port 443 \-redirect\-gw off
ovcp vpn config set \-proto tcp \-port 443 \-redirect\-gw off
ovcp vpn restart
.RE
.fi
Push the internal dnsmasq resolver to clients and block dnsmasq from
forwarding anywhere else \(en internal cert\-hostnames still resolve,
nothing else does:
.nf
.RS
ovcp vpn config set \-push\-internal\-dns on
ovcp dns config set \-block\-internet on
ovcp vpn restart && ovcp dns restart
.RE
.fi
Non\-attended container init with a non\-default subnet and DNS, no CLI
flags or browser involved:
.nf
.RS
OVCP_OPENVPN_SUBNET=10.20.0.0/24 OVCP_OPENVPN_DNS=1.1.1.1,9.9.9.9 \e
  ovcp init \-server\-cn vpn.example.com
.RE
.fi
Scripting: list expired CNs without parsing column output (needs \fBjq\fR):
.nf
.RS
ovcp \-json certificate list | jq \-r '.[] | select(.status == "expired") | .cn'
.RE
.fi
Watch one client's live bandwidth while troubleshooting a slow connection:
.nf
.RS
ovcp stats \-cn dana\-phone \-follow
.RE
.fi
.SH PRIVILEGES
.B ovcp
runs as root and is the sole owner of the PKI and configuration, so those
files are secure by default \(en there is no privilege drop and no
per\-mode juggling. It starts openvpn, which needs root or CAP_NET_ADMIN at
startup and then drops to \fInobody\fR on its own. The \fIpki\fR directory
is \fI0701\fR and \fI$OVCP_DATA\fR itself is \fI0751\fR \(en not
\fI0700\fR/\fI0750\fR \(en so openvpn can still traverse into them after
dropping to \fInobody\fR to re\-stat \fIcrl.pem\fR. Individual files range
from \fI0600\fR (private keys, \fIovcp.db\fR) through \fI0640\fR
(\fIserver.conf\fR) to \fI0644\fR (the CA/server certificates and the CRL,
meant to be publicly verifiable).
In containers, run with \-\-cap\-add NET_ADMIN; the runtime dir
\fI/run/ovcp\fR (0750) holds the management and control sockets. openvpn
runs as a foreground child of ovcp and cannot outlive it (Pdeathsig).
dnsmasq (optional \(en only used if found on \fIPATH\fR) gets the same
treatment: a foreground child, its own privilege drop
(\fBOVCP_DNSMASQ_USER\fR/\fBGROUP\fR), and its own \fI$OVCP_DATA/dns\fR
directory (\fI0750\fR, group\-owned by that account, so \fB\-\-hostsdir\fR
can list it). \fBinit \-skeleton\fR (re)applies every mode above in one
shot, e.g. after fixing a permissions mistake by hand.
.PP
ovcp does not separate the admin UI from the PKI/openvpn\-supervision path
into a lower\-privilege process; see \fBARCHITECTURE.md\fR for the
rationale. The shipped systemd unit sandboxes the whole process instead
(\fBProtectSystem=strict\fR, \fBNoNewPrivileges\fR,
\fBMemoryDenyWriteExecute\fR, and more) \(en native containment, not an
app\-level privilege split.
.SH DEPLOYMENT
One runtime, everywhere: ovcp owns the openvpn worker (fork/exec + signals,
no SIGHUP, no in\-place reload) the same way on bare metal, under systemd,
and inside a container. If ovcp restarts, its \fBserve\fR process brings
openvpn back up.
.SS systemd
A single unit runs ovcp as root; ovcp starts and stops openvpn itself.
Distro packages install \fIovcp.service\fR automatically; a from\-source
build does not (see \fBBUILD.md\fR) and needs it copied in manually:
.nf
.RS
sudo install \-m644 deploy/systemd/ovcp.service /usr/lib/systemd/system/
sudo OVCP_DATA=/var/lib/ovcp ovcp init \-server\-cn vpn.example.com
sudo systemctl enable \-\-now ovcp
.RE
.fi
Overriding \fBOVCP_DATA\fR (e.g. via \fBsystemctl edit ovcp\fR) also needs
the unit's \fBReadWritePaths=\fR updated to match \(en it is a literal
path, not a \fB$OVCP_DATA\fR reference, and \fBProtectSystem=strict\fR
silently blocks writes anywhere else.
.SS container
One all\-in\-one image (\fBmake image\fR): ovcp (as root) owns the PKI and
starts openvpn inside the container. Init once, then run:
.nf
.RS
podman run \-\-rm \-v ovcp:/var/lib/ovcp ovcp init \-server\-cn vpn.example.com
podman run \-d \-\-cap\-add=NET_ADMIN \-\-device /dev/net/tun \e
  \-p 1194:1194/udp \-p 127.0.0.1:8443:8443 \-v ovcp:/var/lib/ovcp ovcp
.RE
.fi
The image defaults \fBOVCP_CA_PASSPHRASE\fR/\fBOVCP_USER_PASSWORD\fR to
\fIchangeme!\fR for the one\-shot init \(en override with \fB\-e\fR. To split
the UI and the VPN across network interfaces, bind each published port to
the right address, e.g.
\fB\-p 203.0.113.7:1194:1194/udp \-p 192.168.1.10:8443:8443\fR. docker works
the same; the Containerfile is plain OCI.
.PP
Client \fBRealAddress\fR (openvpn's own \fBstatus 3\fR/CLIENT_LIST, not
ovcp's HTTP layer) can come back as one container\-internal address for
every client instead of distinct public IPs. Root cause, on any runtime:
a published UDP port routed through a userspace proxy instead of
kernel\-level DNAT replaces the real source address with the proxy's own.
Which runtime does this varies:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDocker\fR: \fBdocker\-proxy\fR (userland\-proxy), on by default. Fix:
run the daemon with \fB\-\-userland\-proxy=false\fR (check via
\fBdocker info | grep \-i userland\fR) \(en pure kernel DNAT, no loss of
network isolation (unlike \fBnetwork_mode: host\fR, not recommended here).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBPodman\fR: rootful is already kernel DNAT (netavark), unaffected.
Rootless defaults to \fBpasta\fR since Podman 5.0, which also preserves
the source address \(en only the older \fBslirp4netns\fR backend (still
selectable, or the default pre\-5.0) has this problem. Fix: drop
\fB\-\-network slirp4netns\fR (let it use the pasta default), or run
rootful.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBKubernetes\fR: kube\-proxy's default \fBCluster\fR traffic policy SNATs
external traffic. Fix: set \fBservice.spec.externalTrafficPolicy: Local\fR.
.RE
.PP
\fBGET /healthz\fR is an unauthenticated, database\-ping health check \(en
point a container/Kubernetes liveness or readiness probe at it.
.SH SELINUX
On SELinux systems the confined
.BR openvpn (8)
domain cannot read a non\-standard data directory. Run
\fIdeploy/selinux.sh\fR once (labels the data dir \fIopenvpn_etc_t\fR and
the logs \fIopenvpn_var_log_t\fR; honors \fB$OVCP_DATA\fR). ovcp itself
runs unconfined and needs no policy.
